2. THE SUBSTRATE AND THE HODGE COMPLEX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 The 5-Point Laplacian Is Forced

Axiom (Lattice Primitive). There exists a finite periodic square lattice
Lambda_L = Z_L x Z_L with L a positive even integer.

Theorem 2.1 (Laplacian Uniqueness). Let Delta: C^N -> C^N be a linear
operator on Lambda_L satisfying (i) D_4-invariance, (ii) nearest-neighbour
locality, (iii) positive-semidefiniteness with ker Delta = constants. Then
Delta is uniquely determined up to scale, and equals

    (Delta f)(x_1, x_2) = 4 f(x_1, x_2) - sum_{|e|=1} f(x+e).

Proof. Write Delta f(x) = a_0 f(x) + sum_mu a_mu f(x+mu). D_4-invariance forces
a_mu = a for all four nearest-neighbour directions. ker Delta = constants
forces a_0 + 4a = 0, hence a_0 = 4a. Normalizing a=1 gives the 5-point
Laplacian.

2.2 The Spectrum and Mirror Symmetry

On plane waves phi_{m_1,m_2}(x) = e^{2 pi i (m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2)/L}, Delta acts
as

    lambda(m_1, m_2) = 4 - 2 cos(2 pi m_1 / L) - 2 cos(2 pi m_2 / L).

Let c_k = cos(k pi / 4). Then lambda = 4 - 2 c_{m_1} - 2 c_{m_2}. The symmetry
c_k <-> c_{4-k} = -c_k gives

    lambda(m_1, m_2) <-> 8 - lambda(m_1, m_2)

under (m_1, m_2) -> (m_1 + 4, m_2 + 4) mod 8. The unique self-paired eigenvalue
is lambda = 4.

Theorem 2.2 (Spectrum). The scalar Laplacian spectrum on Z_8 x Z_8 is

    lambda = 0          (mult 1)
    lambda = 2 - sqrt2  (mult 4)
    lambda = 4 - 2sqrt2 (mult 4)
    lambda = 2          (mult 4)
    lambda = 4 - sqrt2  (mult 8)
    lambda = 2 + sqrt2  (mult 4)
    lambda = 4          (mult 14)
    lambda = 4 + sqrt2  (mult 8)
    lambda = 6 - sqrt2  (mult 4)
    lambda = 4 + 2sqrt2 (mult 4)
    lambda = 6          (mult 4)
    lambda = 6 + sqrt2  (mult 4)
    lambda = 8          (mult 1)

Total multiplicity: 64.

2.3 The 14-Mode Multiplet

The self-paired eigenvalue lambda = 4 has multiplicity 2(L-1) = 14. The 14
modes are

    O_0 = {(0,4), (4,0)},
    O_1 = {(1,3), (1,5), (3,1), (3,7), (5,1), (5,7), (7,3), (7,5)},
    O_2 = {(2,2), (2,6), (6,2), (6,6)}.

Orbit sizes (2, 8, 4).

Proposition 2.3 (Isotypic Decomposition). The 14-mode multiplet decomposes
under D_4 as

    M_14 = 3 A_1 + A_2 + 2 B_1 + 2 B_2 + 3 E.

Proof. Character counting. The permutation character is chi = (14, 2, 0, 2, 2)
on the five conjugacy classes (e, r^2, r, s, rs). Projecting onto each irrep:
n_A1 = 3, n_A2 = 1, n_B1 = 2, n_B2 = 2, n_E = 3. Dimension check:
3 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 6 = 14.

