4. THE FAILURE OF NAIVE LATTICE BRST
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4.1 The Naive BRST Operator

Let c^a(x) be Grassmann-valued 0-forms valued in g, and A_mu^a(x) be real
1-forms. Define

    s A_mu^a(x) = partial_mu c^a(x) + f^{abc} A_mu^b(x) c^c(x),
    s c^a(x) = -1/2 f^{abc} c^b(x) c^c(x),

where partial_mu c^a(x) = c^a(x + e_mu) - c^a(x) is the lattice forward
difference.

4.2 The Structural Failure

Theorem 4.1 (Naive Lattice BRST Fails). For all field configurations,

    s^2 A_mu^a(x) = -1/4 f^{abc} [L_1^x L_1^y (c^b c^c)](x,x) + O(f^2 c^3),

where L_1 is the 1D lattice Laplacian acting on the site index.

Proof. Decompose s A_mu^a = partial_mu c^a + f^{abc} A_mu^b c^c. Then
s(partial_mu c^a) = -1/2 f^{abc} [c^b(x+e_mu) c^c(x+e_mu) - c^b(x) c^c(x)]
(same-site bilinears), while s(f^{abc} A_mu^b c^c) contains
f^{abc} partial_mu c^b(x) c^c(x) (cross-site bilinears). In the continuum
these cancel because partial_mu is a derivation. On the lattice,
partial_mu(c^b c^c) is NOT equal to (partial_mu c^b) c^c + c^b (partial_mu c^c).
The defect is exactly the mixed second derivative L_1^x L_1^y (c^b c^c).

Corollary 4.2. The lattice BRST operator with the naive discrete derivative is
not nilpotent.

4.3 The Substrate's Own Nilpotent Operator

The substrate has a different nilpotent operator: D = d + d*, with D^2 = Delta.
This is the substrate's own BRST-like structure. It is not an imported
continuum BRST operator; it is intrinsic to the Hodge complex.

